From ad38844a5d334b0e83a991ab2ed70a5a89403590 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 22:56:05 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent: hold the agent's task feed and let the thread stop or
 background a task

0029 taught the sidebar that work can outlive the turn that launched it, but
it only ever learned a number: `_meta["_claude/backgroundTasks"].count`. A
card or a history panel needs the tasks themselves -- what each one is, how
long it has run, how it ended -- and a way to act on one without typing a
prompt. This patch is the model and the actions; nothing renders them yet.
Stories 013 (the card, the tray) and 014 (the history panel) consume it.

The contract, carried by claude-agent-acp-plus since the release that added
it (story 012):

- `session_info_update._meta["_claude/tasks"]` = `{ tasks: [...] }`, each
  entry `{ id, toolCallId, type, description, background, depth, status,
  startedAt, endedAt, usage, lastTool, summary }`, status one of running,
  completed, failed, stopped, interrupted. It is a SNAPSHOT, not a delta:
  every update carries every live task, so a lost update is repaired by the
  next one. An ended task appears in exactly one snapshot, then drops out.
- `_claude/tasks/stop { sessionId, taskId } -> {}` and
  `_claude/tasks/background { sessionId, toolCallId } -> { backgrounded }`,
  two JSON-RPC requests sent through `UntypedMessage`.
- The `initialize` response advertises both as
  `agentCapabilities._meta["_claude/tasks"] = { version: 1 }`.

What this adds:

- `AcpThread::tasks()`, replaced wholesale on each snapshot, and two events:
  `TasksUpdated` when the list differs, `TaskEnded { task_id, status }` once
  per ending. The announced set forgets an id when it leaves the list or comes
  back running, because a resumed subagent restarts under its agent id and its
  second ending is news too.
- A payload that does not parse logs a warning and leaves the list alone; an
  update without the key (the adapter's title-only updates ride the same
  notification) changes nothing; a PRESENT empty list clears it.
- A running task missing from the next snapshot, never seen ending, is kept
  once as `interrupted` and announced. When the agent process exits, or the
  session is closed through `AgentConnection::close_session`, every running
  task becomes `interrupted`, once. `AcpConnection` had no `close_session`
  override; the one added waits for an in-flight load or release, closes once,
  then interrupts. `supports_close_session()` stays false, so in practice only
  the exit path is live today.
- `AgentConnection::{stop_task, background_task}`, defaulting to "not
  supported". `AcpConnection` sends them only when the capability above is
  advertised -- never keyed on the entry id or the agent's name, which differ
  between the published adapter and a local fork.
- The retention clause: `is_idle_for_retention()` is false while any task is
  running. A turn that ended with background work is idle by ThreadStatus, so
  without it `cleanup_retained_threads` dropped exactly the threads a task
  card exists to show. `TasksUpdated` also re-runs that cleanup, so a thread
  kept for its tasks is released when the last one ends rather than at the
  next unrelated event.

What it deliberately does not do:

- It leaves 0029's `background_task_count` and its key alone instead of
  deriving the count from the new list. An adapter that sends only the old key
  still lights the sidebar, and the two cannot disagree visibly because the
  adapter publishes both from the same table.
- It routes nothing through ThreadMetadataStore, for the reason 0029 gives.
- It marks nothing on a thread that is being dropped: the thread is destroyed
  with its tasks, so there is no observer to tell.

The wire shape is pinned by a fixture shared byte for byte with the adapter
(`crates/acp_thread/src/test_data/claude_tasks_snapshot.json`, parsed by
`test_tasks_contract_fixture_parses`). No other agent sends the key or the
capability, so for them nothing changes. Rides the claude-agent-acp-plus flag
BY DEPENDENCY, like 0011, 0013 and 0029.
---

